Every item of a nested task list keeps the box it owns, at the level it
was written, under both a task parent and a plain one.

The items of a list reach the writer as ONE flat array across every level
of nesting, so a box that is read once per item and handed to each piece
the item splits into lands on pieces that never had one. Here each level
carries its own state, and the plain parent in the middle keeps no box.

The ordered step gains a blank line above it. Changing the marker starts a
new list in CommonMark, so those last two lines are a second block, and a
blank line is what separates one block from the next.
